==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|haitanan||command, name|lang=gem-pro}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|kei-|lang=ine-pro}}, {{recons|ki-||put in motion, be moving|lang=ine-pro}}. Cognate with Old Frisian {{term|heta|hēta|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|hetan|hētan|lang=osx}}, Old Norse {{term|heita|lang=non}} (Swedish {{term|heta|lang=sv}}), Gothic {{term|𐌷𐌰𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽|tr=haitan|lang=got}}. The Indo-European root is also the source of Ancient Greek {{term|κίειν||tr=kíein|put in motion|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|ciere||rouse, make active|lang=la}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈhɑːtɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|class=VII|head=hātan}} (''3 singular present'' {{Latinx|'''[[hætt|hǣtt]]'''}}, ''3 singular preterite'' '''[[het|hēt]]/[[heht|hēht]]''', ''preterite plural'' '''[[heton|hēton]]/[[hehton|hēhton]]''', ''past participle'' ('''ġe''')'''[[haten|hāten]]''')

# to [[order]], [[command]]
# to [[call]] or [[name]]
# {{intransitive|lang=ang}} to be called or named

